WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:06.380
The trailer for director Nia DaCosta's Candyman,
a "spiritual sequel" to the 1992 horror classic

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:11.219
of the same name, has finally been released
online, and it promises plenty of cerebral

00:00:11.219 --> 00:00:15.990
thrills of the kind that we've come to associate
with producer and co-writer Jordan Peele - not

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:20.460
to mention an inspired take on a story that
sent chills down the spines of horror fans

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:21.720
three decades ago.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.560
These are just a few things that may have
slipped by you in that excellent new trailer.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:31.940
Yahya Abdul-Mateen II portrays Anthony McCoy,
who moves into the neighborhood near Chicago's

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:36.940
Cabrini-Green housing projects, the area which
was once terrorized by Daniel Robitaille - that's

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.780
the titular hook-wielding specter's real name,
by the way.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:44.660
These days, though, it looks a little different;
the area has become near-completely gentrified,

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:48.440
with most of the buildings comprising the
old housing projects having been demolished.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:53.360
In their place are ritzy high-rises, houses,
shops, and art museums - the latter of which

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.230
may be what drew McCoy to the area.

00:00:55.230 --> 00:01:00.199
McCoy, you see, is an artist, just like Robitaille
himself; apparently, he's a pretty talented

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.880
one, as the trailer implies that it doesn't
take him long to score an exhibit in his new

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:04.700
neighborhood.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:08.980
It looks like Candyman will see McCoy becoming
just a wee bit obsessed with Robitaille's

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.200
legend, which is the focus of the exhibit.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.580
Hell, it even includes an arrangement of mirrors.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:16.000
"The mirror invites you to summon him."

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.640
The spot reveals that McCoy begins to identify
with Robitaille just a bit too closely, and

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:24.229
their shared profession raises the question
of how else they might be connected.

00:01:24.229 --> 00:01:26.450
Is McCoy a descendant of Candyman?

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:28.900
Or even the reincarnation of the doomed artist?

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:33.149
In the original film, it was heavily implied
that Helen may have been Caroline Sullivan,

00:01:33.149 --> 00:01:35.090
Robitaille's lover, reincarnated.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:38.420
It's not clear what the connection is - but
it seems certain that there is one.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.440
"Once a troubled corner of Chicago, but the
area that was home to the Cabrini-Green housing

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:46.420
projects has changed pretty drastically over
the years.”

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:51.659
The fact of Cabrini-Green's gentrification
is a fascinating aspect of this new Candyman's

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:56.380
plot, and one which producer and co-writer
Jordan Peele is sure to mine for a truckload

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:57.570
of thematic resonance.

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:01.820
Interestingly, though, the trailer makes it
plain that while the area has undergone quite

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:06.750
the "revitalization," its past isn't completely
buried; fragments of it remain, such as the

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:11.190
old, boarded up buildings where McCoy first
encounters William Burke - played by Colman

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:13.849
Domingo - who appears to be a long-time resident.

00:02:13.849 --> 00:02:18.239
The idea here seems to be that a troubled,
violent past can never truly be buried...

00:02:18.239 --> 00:02:21.110
not even by pricey condos and upscale restaurants.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:22.569
Hey - the truth hurts…

00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:23.060
literally.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:23.740
"Shh!

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:24.240
Shh…

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:25.960
Don't.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.140
Don't say that."

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:32.640
Near the spot's end, as it really starts to
pick up steam with the lightning-quick edits

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:36.590
and disturbing imagery, a voice creeps in
on the soundtrack - and if you're a fan of

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:38.840
the original movie, you recognized it immediately.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.560
"I am the writing on the wall.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:44.460
The sweet smell of blood.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.420
Be... my victim."

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:50.580
Yep, it's the man himself: that's the voice
of Tony Todd, whose restrained yet intense

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:55.260
performance made Candyman such a frightening
presence in the 1992 flick.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:59.120
For a good chunk of the time the film was
in production, it was thought that Abdul-Mateen

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.410
would be replacing Todd as Candyman; obviously,
this isn't the case, but DaCosta isn't particularly

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:08.209
keen to let us know just what the case is,
or exactly how large a part Todd will have

00:03:08.209 --> 00:03:09.239
in the new film.

00:03:09.239 --> 00:03:11.670
In March 2019, the director told Collider:

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:13.290
"It's been slightly misreported.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:17.319
I can't say what's happening in the film because
we want it to be a surprise, but [Abdul-Mateen

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:19.280
is] not replacing Tony Todd.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:22.840
That's been reported, and I was just like,
'I don't know what to say about this.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:23.700
This is not right.'"

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Since we never see Candyman's face in the
trailer - only his bloody hook - we can't

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.320
say with certainty that Todd will reprise
his role.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.400
But he is definitely involved - and that can
only be a good thing.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:38.100
We'll see just what Peele, DaCosta, and company
have cooked up for us when Candyman hits the

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.580
big screen on June 12, 2020.

